Adaptive Plasticity–Routing Fields

A Control-Theoretic Framework for Continually Self-Organizing Modular Intelligence

Version 0.5 — Final Theoretical Manuscript

Author: Ouadi Maakoul

AI : chatGpt, Claude, Deepseek, Meta AI

Abstract

This manuscript presents a mathematical framework for continual learning in modular neural architectures. The Adaptive Plasticity–Routing Field (APRF) is a control layer that coordinates per-expert plasticity, prototype-based sparse routing, slow/fast consolidation, structural expansion, and exploration through a single low-dimensional modulation state. The modulation state evolves under a contraction map driven by decorrelated information-theoretic signals: prediction error, epistemic uncertainty, random-network novelty, expected information gain, reward, queue backlogs, and computational cost. Routing is prototype-based, sparse, and validated by per-expert autoencoders, removing reliance on task labels. Structural expansion follows a probe-based singular-value energy criterion. A spectral Parseval regularizer preserves the effective rank of the neural tangent kernel. Stability is formulated using input-to-state stability, queue-augmented Lyapunov drift, and vector small-gain arguments. The central result is a small-gain boundedness theorem under explicit assumptions. No simulations, code training, or physical experiments have been conducted. All empirical claims are stated as falsifiable hypotheses for future work.

---

1. Introduction

Consider an agent interacting with a partially observable, non-stationary environment. At time t, the agent observes o_t, selects action a_t, and receives reward \rho_t. The environment evolves according to

s_{t+1}=F(s_t,a_t,\xi_t),
\qquad
o_t=H(s_t,\nu_t),

where s_t is the latent environmental state and \xi_t,\nu_t are noise terms.

A conventional learner updates parameters \theta of a fixed function f_\theta. This is sufficient for static tasks but insufficient when the data distribution shifts, tasks appear sequentially, or new skills must be acquired without overwriting prior knowledge.

We model the agent as a modular system:

\hat o_t
=
\sum_{j=1}^{M_t}
\tilde p_j(t)\,
f_{\bar\theta_j}(x_t,a_t),

where M_t is the number of active experts, \tilde p_j(t) are sparse routing probabilities, and \bar\theta_j are neuromodulated parameters. The architecture itself evolves through expert creation and pruning.

The core object is a modulation state

m_t = \Psi_\psi(s_t, m_{t-1}),

where s_t\in\mathbb R^k is a normalized vector of global signals. The map \Psi_\psi is constrained to be a contraction with Lipschitz constant L_\Psi<1. Contraction alone is insufficient for closed-loop stability because s_t depends on m_t. A vector small-gain condition is required.

This manuscript is purely theoretical. No empirical experiments were conducted. The purpose is to define the framework, state assumptions, and prove or formally sketch stability claims.

