2. Notation and Problem Setting

Let (\Omega,\mathcal F,\{\mathcal F_t\}_{t\ge0},\mathbb P) be a filtered probability space. The environment is a partially observable Markov decision process with state s_t\in\mathcal S, action a_t\in\mathcal A, observation o_t\in\mathcal O, and transition

s_{t+1}=F(s_t,a_t,\xi_t),
\qquad
o_t=H(s_t,\nu_t).

The agent maintains internal state x_t\in\mathcal X and cognitive architecture

\mathcal G_t=(\mathcal E_t, W_t, \mathcal M_t),

where

\mathcal E_t=\{\mathcal E_1,\dots,\mathcal E_{M_t}\},
\qquad
W_t=[w_{ij}(t)],

and \mathcal M_t is a memory buffer. We allow

\mathcal G_{t+1}\neq \mathcal G_t.

Each expert is a tuple

\mathcal E_i
=
(z_i,\theta_i,\phi_i,v_i,q_i,\chi_i),

with the following meaning:

· z_i\in\mathbb R^{d_z}: latent state,
· \theta_i\in\mathbb R^{d_\theta}: fast trainable parameters,
· \phi_i\in\mathbb R^{d_\theta}: slow consolidated parameters,
· v_i\in\mathbb R^{d_v}: routing prototype,
· q_i\in[0,1]: competence estimate,
· \chi_i\in[0,1]: stability/plasticity state.

The fast weights \theta_i are updated by gradient steps. The slow weights \phi_i are an exponential moving average:

\phi_i(t+1)
=
\beta_i(t)\phi_i(t)
+
(1-\beta_i(t))\theta_i(t),

where \beta_i(t)\in[\beta_{\min},\beta_{\max}] is a consolidation coefficient.

The effective parameters used for inference are

\bar\theta_i(t)
=
\alpha_i^{\mathrm{fast}}(t)\theta_i(t)
+
\alpha_i^{\mathrm{slow}}(t)\phi_i(t),

with

\alpha_i^{\mathrm{fast}}(t)+\alpha_i^{\mathrm{slow}}(t)=1,
\qquad
\alpha_i^{\mathrm{fast}}(t),\alpha_i^{\mathrm{slow}}(t)\in[0,1].

