3. Information-Theoretic Modulation Signals

Define the normalized signal vector

s_t
=
\mathrm{LayerNorm}\!\left(
[e_t,\ U_t,\ \nu_t,\ I_t,\ \rho_t,\ H_t^Q,\ \kappa_t]^\top
\right)
\in\mathbb R^{k}.

The components are chosen to be decorrelated by construction. In particular, H_t^Q is a queue-based homeostatic signal, not a raw weight norm.

3.1 Prediction Error

e_t
=
\|o_t-\hat o_t\|_{\Sigma^{-1}}^2,

where \Sigma is an exponentially weighted covariance of recent residuals:

\Sigma_{t+1}
=
(1-\tau)\Sigma_t
+
\tau\,(o_t-\hat o_t)(o_t-\hat o_t)^\top,
\qquad
\tau\in(0,1).

3.2 Epistemic Uncertainty

Let q_t(\theta) be an approximate posterior over fast weights. Define

U_t
=
\mathbb E_{q_t}\!\left[
D_{KL}\!\left(q_t(\theta)\,\|\,p(\theta)\right)
\right],

where p(\theta) is a prior. In a deterministic Laplace approximation,

U_t
\approx
\frac{1}{2}
\log\det\!\left(
\nabla_\theta^2\mathcal L_t+\lambda I
\right)^{-1}.

3.3 Novelty via Random Network Distillation

To avoid the noisy-TV failure mode, novelty is defined through random network distillation:

\nu_t
=
\|f_{\mathrm{rand}}(o_t)-f_{\mathrm{pred}}(o_t)\|^2,

where f_{\mathrm{rand}} is a fixed randomly initialized network and f_{\mathrm{pred}} is a predictor trained on past observations.

3.4 Expected Information Gain

For an action a_t,

I_t
=
\mathbb E_{o_{t+1}\sim p(\cdot|a_t)}\!\left[
D_{KL}\!\left(
p(\theta|o_{\le t},a_t,o_{t+1})
\,\|\,
p(\theta|o_{\le t},a_t)
\right)
\right].

3.5 Reward

\rho_t=\rho(s_t,a_t).

If no external reward is available, a proxy such as negative free energy may be used.

3.6 Homeostatic Queue Signal

Let Q_j(t) be compute queues and P_i(t) plasticity queues, defined in Section 7. Define

H_t^Q
=
\tanh\!\left(
\sum_{j=1}^{N_r} Q_j(t)
+
\sum_{i=1}^{M_t} P_i(t)
\right).

This reflects resource backlogs without double counting parameter drift.

3.7 Computational Cost

For each resource j,

\kappa_j(t)
=
\sum_{i=1}^{M_t}
c_{ij}\,
\mathbf 1\{\tilde p_i(t)>0\},

where c_{ij} is the cost of expert i in resource j. The total cost is

\kappa_t=\sum_{j=1}^{N_r}\kappa_j(t).

