4. The Adaptive Plasticity–Routing Field

The controller is a spectral-normalized recurrent map

m_t
=
\Psi_\psi(s_t,m_{t-1})
=
\tanh\!\left(
W_s s_t+W_m m_{t-1}+b
\right),

with constraints

\|W_m\|_2\le L_\Psi<1,
\qquad
\|W_s\|_2\le L_s.

This implies the Lipschitz condition

\|\Psi_\psi(s,m)-\Psi_\psi(s',m')\|
\le
L_s\|s-s'\|+L_\Psi\|m-m'\|.

The controller outputs several modulation quantities.

4.1 Per-Expert Plasticity

\eta_i(t)
=
\eta_{\min}
+
(\eta_{\max}-\eta_{\min})
\sigma\!\left(
w_\eta^\top[m_t;\,\chi_i(t);\,q_i(t)]
\right)
\in[\eta_{\min},\eta_{\max}].

4.2 Fast/Slow Blending

\alpha_i^{\mathrm{fast}}(t)
=
\sigma\!\left(
w_{\mathrm{fast}}^\top[m_t;\,z_i(t);\,q_i(t)]
\right),

and

\alpha_i^{\mathrm{slow}}(t)
=
1-\alpha_i^{\mathrm{fast}}(t).

4.3 Routing Temperature

T(m_t)
=
\mathrm{softplus}\!\left(w_T^\top m_t\right)
+
T_{\min}.

4.4 Exploration Coefficient

\lambda_t
=
\sigma\!\left(
\alpha_U U_t+\alpha_\nu\nu_t+\alpha_I I_t-\alpha_R\rho_t
\right).

4.5 Consolidation Coefficient

\beta_i(t)
=
\beta_{\min}
+
(\beta_{\max}-\beta_{\min})
\sigma\!\left(
w_\beta^\top[m_t;\,\chi_i(t)]
\right).

---

5. Prototype-Based Sparse Routing with Autoencoder Validation

Global routers are a single point of failure in continual learning. We use prototype-based routing with sparse encoding and task-agnostic validation.

5.1 Sparse Encoding

Let f_t=h(o_t) be a feature vector from a shared encoder. Define

g=\mathrm{SinusoidalGrid}(f_t),

and

z_{\mathrm{sep}}
=
\mathrm{TopK}\!\left(
\mathrm{LayerNorm}\!\left(
\mathrm{ReLU}\!\left(W_{DG}\,g\right)
\right),
\,
0.05\,d
\right),

where d=\dim(z_{\mathrm{sep}}) and TopK retains the largest 0.05\,d entries. The query vector is

u_t=z_{\mathrm{sep}}.

5.2 Prototype Similarity and Sparse Gating

For each expert j,

s_j(t)=v_j^\top u_t.

The raw routing probability is

p_j(t)
=
\frac{
\exp\!\left(s_j(t)/T(m_t)\right)
}{
\sum_{k=1}^{M_t}
\exp\!\left(s_k(t)/T(m_t)\right)
}.

Let

K=\max(1,\lfloor\rho M_t\rfloor),
\qquad
\rho\in(0,1].

The sparse routing probability is

\tilde p_j(t)
=
\frac{
p_j(t)\,
\mathbf 1\{j\in\mathrm{TopK}(p(t),K)\}
}{
\sum_{k\in\mathrm{TopK}}p_k(t)
}.

5.3 Autoencoder Validation

Maintain a lightweight autoencoder AE_j for each expert. During inference, if

\|u_t-AE_j(u_t)\|^2>\tau_{AE},

then set p_j(t)=0 before top-K gating. This provides task-agnostic routing without labels.

5.4 Routing Regularization

Define

\mathcal L_{\mathrm{route}}
=
\lambda_{\mathrm{orth}}
\sum_{i\neq j}
(v_i^\top v_j)^2
+
\lambda_{\mathrm{spec}}
\left\|
W_{DG}^\top W_{DG}-I
\right\|_F^2.

The first term enforces quasi-orthogonality of prototypes. The second is a Parseval regularization preserving singular values near one.

