9. Proposed Empirical Protocol

No empirical experiments have been conducted. This section specifies a prospective protocol for future validation.

9.1 Benchmarks

· Random Label CIFAR-10/MNIST: 100% random labels, train to zero error.
· Shuffle CIFAR-10/Mini-ImageNet.
· Split CIFAR-100/ImageNet.
· Sequential 2D navigation.
· Continual World CW10/CW20.

9.2 Baselines

Matched parameter count and inference FLOPs:

1. Fixed monolithic network.
2. Static mixture-of-experts.
3. HiCL DG-gated MoE.
4. NeuMoSync.
5. Routing-Based Continual Learning with LoRA experts.
6. Stable-MoE.
7. SPHERE spectral plasticity regularizer.
8. Fixed-modulation APRF.
9. No-structural-adaptation APRF.
10. Dense routing control.
11. No-SPHERE control.

9.3 Metrics

· T_{\mathrm{adapt}}=\min\{t:e_t<\epsilon\}.
· F_{\mathrm{cat}}=A_{\mathrm{old}}-A_{\mathrm{after}}.
· Forward and backward transfer.
· Effective rank r_e(\mathbf K_t) over tasks.
· Structural efficiency \Delta A/\Delta M.
· Computational efficiency \Delta A/\Delta\mathrm{FLOP}.

9.4 Falsifiable Claim

The central hypothesis is:

APRF maintains r_e(\mathbf K_t)\ge r_{\min} and queue stability while reducing T_{\mathrm{adapt}} by more than 20% and F_{\mathrm{cat}} by more than 30% compared to routing-based continual learning with LoRA experts without modulation, at matched FLOPs.

---

10. Conclusion

We have defined the Adaptive Plasticity–Routing Field, a control-theoretic layer for modular continual learning. The framework replaces independent controllers with a shared contraction map driven by decorrelated signals. Routing is prototype-based, sparse, and validated without task labels. Structural growth uses probe-based singular-value energy. Learning is spectrally regularized to preserve effective rank. Stability is stated as a vector small-gain theorem with queue-augmented Lyapunov drift.

This manuscript is purely theoretical. No simulations, code training, or physical experiments were performed. The next scientific step is to implement the framework and test the falsifiable claim on the proposed benchmarks.

