Appendix C. Proof of Lemma 2: Fast-Weight Input-to-State Stability

Lemma 2. For each expert i, let the local loss

\mathcal L_i(\theta)=\tilde p_i(t)\,\ell(o_t,f_\theta(x_t,a_t))+\lambda_{\mathrm{SPHERE}}\mathcal L_{\mathrm{spec},i}(\theta)

be \mu_i-strongly convex and L_i-smooth in a neighborhood of a local minimizer \theta_i^*. Let the routing probability \tilde p_i(t) and modulation state m_t be bounded exogenous inputs. If

\eta_i(t)\in[\eta_{\min},\eta_{\max}],
\qquad
\eta_{\max}<\frac{2}{L_i+\mu_i},

then the fast-weight iteration

\theta_i(t+1)=\theta_i(t)-\eta_i(t)\nabla_{\theta_i}\mathcal L_i(\theta_i(t))

is input-to-state stable with respect to perturbations caused by routing mismatch and modulation. Specifically, there exist \rho_i(\eta)\in(0,1) and \gamma_i\ge0 such that

\|\theta_i(t+1)-\theta_i^*\|
\le
\rho_i(\eta)\|\theta_i(t)-\theta_i^*\|
+
\gamma_i\Bigl(\|m_t\|+\|\tilde p_i(t)-p_i^*\|+\varepsilon_0\Bigr),

where p_i^* is the target routing probability and \varepsilon_0\ge0 is a bounded gradient residual at \theta_i^*.

Proof. Denote \theta=\theta_i(t), \theta^+=\theta_i(t+1), and \eta=\eta_i(t). The gradient descent step is

\theta^+
=
\theta-\eta\nabla\mathcal L_i(\theta).

Let \theta_i^* be a local minimizer, so that \nabla\mathcal L_i(\theta_i^*) is not necessarily zero because the loss depends on \tilde p_i(t) and m_t. We write

\nabla\mathcal L_i(\theta)
=
\nabla\mathcal L_i^*(\theta)
+
r_t,

where \mathcal L_i^* is the loss evaluated at the target routing p_i^* and zero modulation, and r_t captures the effect of routing mismatch and modulation:

r_t
=
\nabla_{\theta}\Bigl[
(\tilde p_i(t)-p_i^*)\,\ell(o_t,f_\theta)
+
\lambda_{\mathrm{SPHERE}}\Bigl(\mathcal L_{\mathrm{spec},i}(m_t)-\mathcal L_{\mathrm{spec},i}(0)\Bigr)
\Bigr].

Under smoothness and boundedness assumptions, there exists \gamma_i\ge0 such that

\|r_t\|
\le
\gamma_i\Bigl(\|\tilde p_i(t)-p_i^*\|+\|m_t\|\Bigr).

Let g(\theta)=\nabla\mathcal L_i^*(\theta). Since \mathcal L_i^* is \mu_i-strongly convex and L_i-smooth,

\|\theta-\eta g(\theta)-(\theta_i^*-\eta g(\theta_i^*))\|
\le
\rho_i(\eta)\|\theta-\theta_i^*\|,

with

\rho_i(\eta)
=
\max\Bigl\{|1-\eta\mu_i|,\ |1-\eta L_i|\Bigr\}.

If \eta_{\max}<\min\{2/\mu_i,2/L_i\}=2/L_i because L_i\ge\mu_i, then \rho_i(\eta)<1. The stronger condition \eta_{\max}<2/(L_i+\mu_i) certainly implies \eta_{\max}<2/L_i, so \rho_i<1.

Now

\theta^+
=
\theta-\eta\bigl(g(\theta)+r_t\bigr).

Subtracting \theta_i^*,

\theta^+-\theta_i^*
=
\theta-\theta_i^*
-
\eta g(\theta)
-
\eta r_t
+
\eta g(\theta_i^*)
-
\eta g(\theta_i^*)

=
\Bigl[\theta-\theta_i^*-\eta\bigl(g(\theta)-g(\theta_i^*)\bigr)\Bigr]
-
\eta\bigl(r_t+g(\theta_i^*)\bigr).

Taking norms,

\|\theta^+-\theta_i^*\|
\le
\rho_i(\eta)\|\theta-\theta_i^*\|
+
\eta\|r_t+g(\theta_i^*)\|.

By the triangle inequality,

\eta\|r_t+g(\theta_i^*)\|
\le
\eta\gamma_i\Bigl(\|\tilde p_i(t)-p_i^*\|+\|m_t\|\Bigr)
+
\eta\|g(\theta_i^*)\|.

Define

\varepsilon_0=\max_t \eta_i(t)\|g(\theta_i^*)\|,

which is bounded. Then

\|\theta^+-\theta_i^*\|
\le
\rho_i(\eta)\|\theta-\theta_i^*\|
+
\gamma_i\Bigl(\|\tilde p_i(t)-p_i^*\|+\|m_t\|+\varepsilon_0\Bigr),

possibly after enlarging \gamma_i. This establishes the ISS property. \square

