Appendix D. Proof of Theorem 1: Small-Gain Boundedness

Theorem 1. Under assumptions (A1)–(A6) and the small-gain condition

\rho(\Gamma)<1,
\qquad
L_\Psi\cdot\mathcal G_{m\to s}<1,

we have

\sup_{t}\mathbb E\bigl[\|X_t\|^2\bigr]<\infty,

and queue mean-rate stability:

\lim_{t\to\infty}\frac{\mathbb E\bigl[\sum_{j=1}^{N_r}Q_j(t)\bigr]}{t}=0.

Proof structure. We decompose the full state X_t into interconnected ISS subsystems.

D.1 Subsystem description

Define

e_i^\theta(t)=\theta_i(t)-\theta_i^*,
\qquad
e_i^\phi(t)=\phi_i(t)-\theta_i^*.

The fast-weight subsystem is ISS by Lemma 2:

\|e_i^\theta(t+1)\|
\le
\rho_i\|e_i^\theta(t)\|
+
\gamma_i\|u_i(t)\|,

where u_i(t) collects routing mismatch and modulation effects.

The slow-weight update is

\phi_i(t+1)
=
\beta_i(t)\phi_i(t)+(1-\beta_i(t))\theta_i(t).

Since \beta_i(t)\in[0,1],

\|\phi_i(t+1)-\theta_i^*\|
=
\|\beta_i(t)(\phi_i(t)-\theta_i^*) + (1-\beta_i(t))(\theta_i(t)-\theta_i^*)\|
\le
\max\{\|\phi_i(t)-\theta_i^*\|,\ \|\theta_i(t)-\theta_i^*\|\}.

Hence the slow-weight error is ISS with zero gain from \|e_i^\theta\|.

The routing subsystem is bounded because prototypes v_i are updated as convex combinations of bounded queries u_t, and the sparse gating function is Lipschitz in m_t with bounded gain. The autoencoder validation only zeroes certain probabilities, so it does not increase gains.

The modulation state m_t evolves by

m_t=\Psi_\psi(s_t,m_{t-1}),

with

\|\Psi_\psi(s,m)-\Psi_\psi(s',m')\|
\le
L_s\|s-s'\|+L_\Psi\|m-m'\|.

By (A1), L_\Psi<1.

The signal vector s_t is a bounded function of prediction error, uncertainty, novelty, information gain, reward, queue backlogs, and cost. Under (A5) and bounded internal states, there exists a gain \mathcal G_{m\to s} such that

\|s_t\|
\le
\mathcal G_{m\to s}\|m_t\|
+
C_s.

The queues Q_j,P_i are stable in the mean-rate sense if their arrivals are bounded and the drift inequality of Lemma 1 holds.

D.2 Interconnection gain matrix

We assemble the subsystem gains into a matrix \Gamma. The entries of \Gamma are products of:

· fast-weight ISS gains \gamma_i,
· slow-weight convex gains (at most 1),
· routing gains,
· controller gains L_s,L_\Psi,
· signal gains \mathcal G_{m\to s}.

The vector small-gain theorem (Jiang et al., 1994, 1996) states that if the spectral radius \rho(\Gamma)<1, then the interconnection is ISS with a linear state-space representation. In particular, the composed map from X_t to X_{t+1} is contractive on average.

D.3 Boundedness in mean square

Let V(X_t)=\|X_t\|^2. Under contraction of the composed map and bounded exogenous noise, there exist \alpha\in(0,1) and C_V>0 such that

\mathbb E\bigl[\|X_{t+1}\|^2\bigr]
\le
\alpha\,\mathbb E\bigl[\|X_t\|^2\bigr]
+
C_V.

Iterating,

\mathbb E\bigl[\|X_t\|^2\bigr]
\le
\alpha^t \mathbb E\bigl[\|X_0\|^2\bigr]
+
\frac{C_V}{1-\alpha}.

Therefore

\sup_t \mathbb E\bigl[\|X_t\|^2\bigr]<\infty.

D.4 Queue mean-rate stability

From Lemma 1,

\Delta\mathcal L_Q(t)
\le
C
-
\sum_{j=1}^{N_r}Q_j(t)\bigl(\bar\kappa_j-\kappa_j(t)\bigr)
-
\sum_{i=1}^{M_t}P_i(t)\bigl(\delta_i^{\max}-\|\theta_i(t+1)-\theta_i(t)\|^2\bigr).

Taking expectations conditioned on \mathcal F_t,

\mathbb E\bigl[\mathcal L_Q(t+1)-\mathcal L_Q(t)\mid\mathcal F_t\bigr]
\le
C
-
\sum_{j=1}^{N_r}Q_j(t)\mathbb E\bigl[\bar\kappa_j-\kappa_j(t)\mid\mathcal F_t\bigr]
-
\sum_{i=1}^{M_t}P_i(t)\mathbb E\bigl[\delta_i^{\max}-\|\theta_i(t+1)-\theta_i(t)\|^2\mid\mathcal F_t\bigr].

Under the stability of the fast-weight dynamics, the expected plasticity queue arrivals are bounded by \delta_i^{\max} on average. Similarly, the routing policy ensures that expected compute arrivals do not exceed budgets \bar\kappa_j. Hence the drift is negative for large queues, implying stability. By a standard Lyapunov drift argument for queues,

\lim_{t\to\infty}\frac{\mathbb E\bigl[\sum_{j=1}^{N_r}Q_j(t)\bigr]}{t}=0.

This completes the proof sketch. \square

