Appendix E. Derivation of Probe-Based Novel Subspace Estimation

We present the derivation of the singular-value energy criterion for structural adaptation.

E.1 Problem statement

Let \mathcal D_k be a data window representing a new task or distribution shift. We fit a low-rank probe adapter A_{\mathrm{probe}}\in\mathbb R^{d_1\times d_2} of rank r_{\max} on \mathcal D_k, keeping all previously learned adapters frozen.

Let

A_{\mathrm{hist}}=[A_1,A_2,\dots,A_{M_{k-1}}]

be the concatenation of historical adapter matrices. Its column space spans the skills already acquired.

E.2 Removing historical subspace

Compute the QR decomposition

A_{\mathrm{hist}}=Q_{\mathrm{hist}}R_{\mathrm{hist}},

where Q_{\mathrm{hist}} has orthonormal columns. The orthogonal projection onto the historical subspace is Q_{\mathrm{hist}}Q_{\mathrm{hist}}^\top. The residual component of the probe adapter outside this subspace is

A_{\mathrm{diff}}
=
A_{\mathrm{probe}}(I-Q_{\mathrm{hist}}^\top Q_{\mathrm{hist}})
=
A_{\mathrm{probe}}-A_{\mathrm{probe}}Q_{\mathrm{hist}}Q_{\mathrm{hist}}^\top.

This projects away directions already representable by existing experts.

E.3 Singular-value decomposition and energy threshold

Let

A_{\mathrm{diff}}=U\Sigma V^\top,
\qquad
\Sigma=\mathrm{diag}(\sigma_1,\dots,\sigma_{r_{\max}}),

with singular values ordered decreasingly. The squared Frobenius norm is

\|A_{\mathrm{diff}}\|_F^2
=
\sum_{j=1}^{r_{\max}}\sigma_j^2.

We seek the smallest r_{\mathrm{new}} such that the rank-r_{\mathrm{new}} truncation captures at least a fraction \theta_{\mathrm{energy}} of the total residual energy:

\frac{\sum_{j=1}^{r_{\mathrm{new}}}\sigma_j^2}{\sum_{j=1}^{r_{\max}}\sigma_j^2}
\ge
\theta_{\mathrm{energy}}.

This is a standard principal component energy criterion. It selects the minimal rank needed to represent the novel directions in the probe adapter beyond what is already captured by historical experts.

E.4 Creation decision

If r_{\mathrm{new}}=0, the probe contains no significant novel directions, and no new expert is created. If r_{\mathrm{new}}>0, the residual contains novel subspace energy above the threshold. The creation gate additionally requires persistent prediction error, small gradient norm, and available budget. This prevents expansion on transient noise or purely random perturbations.

---

Appendix F. Spectral Regularization and Effective Rank

F.1 Parseval regularization

For a weight matrix W\in\mathbb R^{d_{\mathrm{out}}\times d_{\mathrm{in}}}, define

\mathcal L_{\mathrm{Parseval}}(W)=\|W^\top W-I\|_F^2.

Let W=U\Sigma V^\top be the singular value decomposition. If d_{\mathrm{out}}\ge d_{\mathrm{in}}, then

\mathcal L_{\mathrm{Parseval}}(W)
=
\sum_{i=1}^{d_{\mathrm{in}}}\bigl(\sigma_i^2-1\bigr)^2.

Thus minimizing the Parseval loss pushes all singular values toward 1.

F.2 Effective rank

Let \mathbf K_t be the neural tangent kernel of the active network. Let its singular values be \lambda_1,\dots,\lambda_n, with

p_i=\frac{\lambda_i}{\sum_{j=1}^n\lambda_j}.

The effective rank is

r_e(\mathbf K_t)
=
\exp\!\left(-\sum_{i=1}^n p_i\log p_i\right).

If all singular values are equal, p_i=1/n, and

r_e=\exp\!\left(-\sum_{i=1}^n \frac1n\log\frac1n\right)
=
\exp(\log n)
=
n.

If one singular value dominates, r_e approaches 1.

F.3 Relation to spectral diversity

The spectral regularizer applied to each layer encourages the singular values of layer weights to remain near 1. Since the neural tangent kernel is formed by products of Jacobians, near-isometric layer weights preserve the conditioning of the Jacobian products. Consequently, the singular values of \mathbf K_t remain distributed, preventing collapse of the effective rank.

Formally, let J_l be the Jacobian of layer l with respect to its parameters. If the layer weight matrices satisfy W_l^\top W_l\approx I, then

J_lJ_l^\top\approx c I,

for some layer-dependent constant c. The NTK

\mathbf K_t=\sum_l J_lJ_l^\top

then has singular values bounded away from zero, so r_e(\mathbf K_t)\ge r_{\min}.

This motivates the assumption (A6) in the stability theorem.

