Appendix G. Full APRF Algorithm Pseudocode

We present the complete inference and learning loop.

Algorithm 1: APRF

Input: pretrained encoder h, memory buffer \mathcal M, budget B_{\max}, thresholds \tau_C,\tau_{AE},\tau_{\mathrm{prune}},\delta,\theta_{\mathrm{energy}}.

1. Initialize expert set \mathcal E_1 with fast weights \theta_1, slow weights \phi_1=\theta_1, prototype v_1, competence q_1=1, stability \chi_1=0.5.
2. Initialize queues Q_j(0)=0, P_i(0)=0.
3. for each time step t=0,1,2,\dots do
   1. Observe o_t, select action a_t.
   2. Compute feature f_t=h(o_t).
   3. Compute sparse encoding u_t via sinusoidal grid, W_{DG}, TopK, LayerNorm.
   4. for each expert j do
      1. Compute similarity s_j(t)=v_j^\top u_t.
      2. Validate with autoencoder: if \|u_t-AE_j(u_t)\|^2>\tau_{AE}, set p_j(t)=0.
   5. Compute routing probabilities p_j(t) with temperature T(m_t).
   6. Apply top-K gating to get \tilde p_j(t).
   7. Compute output \hat o_t=\sum_{j}\tilde p_j(t)f_{\bar\theta_j}(x_t,a_t).
   8. Compute modulation signals e_t,U_t,\nu_t,I_t,\rho_t,H_t^Q,\kappa_t.
   9. Update modulation state m_t=\Psi_\psi(s_t,m_{t-1}).
   10. For each selected expert j\in\mathcal I_t, update prototype v_j.
   11. For each expert i, update fast weights \theta_i and slow weights \phi_i.
   12. Update communication weights w_{ij}.
   13. Update competence q_i(t+1) and stability \chi_i(t+1).
   14. Update queues Q_j(t+1), P_i(t+1).
   15. If structural creation conditions met, estimate r_{\mathrm{new}} and create expert.
   16. Prune experts with q_i<\tau_{\mathrm{prune}} persistently.
   17. Update memory buffer \mathcal M_t.
   18. Select next action a_{t+1} using exploration coefficient \lambda_t.
4. end for

