Appendix H. Additional Remarks on Signal Decorrelation and Implementation

H.1 Decorrelation of modulation signals

The input to the controller is

s_t=\mathrm{LayerNorm}([e_t,U_t,\nu_t,I_t,\rho_t,H_t^Q,\kappa_t]^\top).

Layer normalization standardizes each component to approximately zero mean and unit variance over a batch or running statistics. This reduces the ill-conditioning caused by different scales.

The queue-based homeostatic signal H_t^Q=\tanh(\sum_j Q_j+\sum_i P_i) is bounded in [-1,1], preventing it from dominating the vector.

H.2 Noisy-TV robustness

The RND novelty signal

\nu_t=\|f_{\mathrm{rand}}(o_t)-f_{\mathrm{pred}}(o_t)\|^2

uses a fixed random target network, so irreducible stochastic observations yield a nonzero but stable novelty floor rather than unbounded prediction error. This prevents the APRF from indefinitely increasing plasticity on unpredictable observations.

H.3 Autoencoder validation

Per-expert autoencoders are trained on the sparse queries assigned to that expert. At inference, a high reconstruction error indicates that the query is outside the expert's typical input distribution, and the expert is excluded from routing. This provides task-agnostic gating without requiring explicit task identity.

